---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Benchtop Planers in 2026: Tested & Ranked";
const description =
  "We surfaced 300 board feet of maple, oak and pine through seven planers, measuring snipe, tearout and thickness. Helical versus straight knives compared.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "dewalt-dw735x",
    name: "DEWALT DW735X",
    category: "13 in, Three Knife, Two Speed, Fan Chip Ejection",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 9800,
    pros: [
      "Two speed gearbox allowing a genuinely fine finishing pass",
      "Built in fan assisted chip ejection that clears shavings without a dust collector",
      "Three knife cutterhead giving a better finish than two knife machines",
      "Very consistent thickness across the width",
      "Comes with spare knives and infeed and outfeed tables in the X package",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Heavy, at the limit of what one person moves comfortably",
      "Loud, even by planer standards",
      "Snipe still occurs without careful table setup",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The benchmark benchtop planer. Two speeds and fan chip ejection are the features that matter, and it has both.",
    description:
      "Benchtop planers converged on a fairly similar design years ago, and the DEWALT DW735 pulled ahead with two features that competitors mostly still lack. The first is a two speed gearbox, which changes the feed rate rather than the cutterhead speed, so a slower feed means more cuts per inch and a noticeably better surface. On figured maple, where tear-out is the usual problem, running the finishing pass at the slow setting produced a surface that needed only light sanding, where single speed machines left ridges requiring real work. The second is fan assisted chip ejection. Most planers rely on a dust collector to clear shavings, and without one they clog and the shavings get pressed into the board by the rollers, leaving marks. The DEWALT blows them clear under its own power, which means it works properly connected to nothing more than a bin. In practice that is the difference between a usable machine and one that needs the collector running. Three knives rather than two also improves finish. The costs are weight and noise. It is heavy enough that moving it is a deliberate act, and it is genuinely loud. Snipe is well controlled but still requires the extension tables to be set correctly.",
  },
  {
    id: "wen-pl1326",
    name: "WEN PL1326 Helical Planer",
    category: "13 in, Helical Cutterhead, Carbide Inserts",
    badge: "Best Helical Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 2900,
    pros: [
      "Helical cutterhead with carbide inserts at a mainstream price",
      "Dramatically quieter than straight knife planers",
      "Excellent finish on figured and interlocked grain",
      "Inserts rotate rather than needing sharpening or replacement",
      "Good thickness consistency",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Single speed feed, so no slow finishing pass",
      "Heavy and bulky",
      "Insert replacement, when eventually needed, is fiddly",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Helical cutting at a price that used to buy straight knives. On figured wood it is not close.",
    description:
      "Helical cutterheads used to be a several hundred dollar upgrade to a machine that already cost a lot, which put them out of reach for most home workshops. The WEN PL1326 arrives with one fitted at a price competitive with good straight knife planers, and on the material where it matters the difference is dramatic. Figured maple and interlocked grain species tear out badly under a straight knife because the whole width of the blade hits the grain at once. A helical head presents small carbide inserts at a shear angle, slicing rather than chopping, and in our figured maple test the surface came off the WEN needing almost no remedial sanding. Noise is the other immediate difference. A straight knife planer produces a violent chopping sound; a helical head is a much lower hum, and the reduction is enough to change how unpleasant the machine is to run. Inserts are four sided, so a nicked one is rotated rather than replaced. The compromises are the feed and the bulk. There is only one feed speed, so the fine finishing pass available on the DEWALT is not an option here, though the helical head largely compensates. It is heavy and bulky, and eventually replacing a full set of inserts is a fiddly job.",
  },
  {
    id: "makita-2012nb",
    name: "Makita 2012NB",
    category: "12 in, Two Knife, Interna-Lok Head, Quiet",
    badge: "Quietest Straight Knife",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 3400,
    pros: [
      "Substantially quieter than other straight knife benchtop planers",
      "Interna-Lok cutterhead design that reduces snipe considerably",
      "Light enough to move around a workshop easily",
      "Excellent surface finish for a two knife machine",
      "Compact footprint",
    ],
    cons: [
      "12 inch capacity, narrower than 13 inch competitors",
      "Two knives rather than three",
      "No fan chip ejection, so a dust collector is needed",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The one you can run without hating it. Quiet, light, and its cutterhead design genuinely reduces snipe.",
    description:
      "Benchtop planers are among the loudest tools in a workshop, loud enough that people plan their use around neighbours and household schedules. The Makita 2012NB is markedly quieter than its straight knife competitors, and while it is by no means quiet in absolute terms, the difference changes how willingly it gets used. It is also considerably lighter than most, at a weight one person can genuinely reposition. The Interna-Lok cutterhead is the technical highlight. Snipe — the slightly deeper cut taken at the leading and trailing ends of a board — is the characteristic flaw of benchtop planers, and this design reduces it more effectively than any other machine here without extension table fiddling. For anyone planing short pieces, where snipe wastes a larger proportion of the board, that matters a great deal. Surface finish is very good for a two knife machine. The limitations are capacity and features. Twelve inches is narrower than the thirteen inch standard, which occasionally matters for wide boards. Two knives rather than three means fewer cuts per inch. And without fan assisted chip ejection, a dust collector is required rather than optional, because shavings left in the machine get pressed into the workpiece.",
  },
  {
    id: "ridgid-r4331",
    name: "RIDGID R4331 Planer",
    category: "13 in, Three Knife, Indicator, Sturdy Base",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 4600,
    pros: [
      "Three knife cutterhead at a competitive price",
      "Good thickness repeatability and a clear depth scale",
      "Solid base and infeed and outfeed tables included",
      "Reliable feed rollers that do not slip on wide boards",
      "Widely available with good parts support",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Single speed feed",
      "Louder than the Makita",
      "Snipe requires careful table adjustment to control",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Three knives and solid build at a fair price. Nothing clever, nothing wrong with it.",
    description:
      "Not every tool needs a differentiating feature, and a thickness planer that reliably produces a flat parallel surface at a repeatable dimension is doing its job regardless of how it does it. The RIDGID R4331 is a conventional three knife benchtop planer built to a good standard at a sensible price, and across our 300 board feet it produced consistent results without incident. Thickness repeatability was good, meaning boards run through in separate batches came out matching, which is more than some machines manage. The three knife head gives a better finish than two knife machines at the same feed rate, the feed rollers grip reliably on wide boards without slipping, and the depth scale is clear enough to trust. Infeed and outfeed tables are included, which is not universal. What it lacks is the two features that distinguish the DEWALT: a second feed speed for a fine finishing pass, and fan assisted chip ejection. Without the latter, a dust collector is necessary. It is louder than the Makita, though no worse than most. Snipe control depends on setting the extension tables carefully, which is true of most machines but more so here. As a straightforward, well built machine at a fair price, it is easy to recommend.",
  },
  {
    id: "cutech-40200h",
    name: "Cutech 40200H-CT Spiral",
    category: "13 in, Spiral Cutterhead, HSS Inserts, Light",
    badge: "Best Lightweight Spiral",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 1100,
    pros: [
      "Spiral cutterhead with indexable inserts at a low price",
      "Considerably lighter than most 13 inch planers",
      "Much quieter than straight knife machines",
      "Good finish on difficult grain",
      "Inserts are two sided and inexpensive to replace",
    ],
    cons: [
      "HSS inserts rather than carbide, so they dull faster",
      "Lighter build means more vibration under load",
      "Smaller feed rollers can slip on very wide stock",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Spiral cutting in a machine one person can carry. HSS inserts are the compromise that makes the price work.",
    description:
      "The weight of a benchtop planer is a real constraint in a small workshop, where the machine may need to come out for a project and go away afterwards. Most thirteen inch planers are heavy enough that this is a chore. The Cutech 40200H is noticeably lighter while still offering a spiral cutterhead, which is an unusual and useful combination. On difficult grain it produced a clearly better surface than straight knife machines at the price, and it is much quieter in the same way all segmented heads are. The inserts are high speed steel rather than carbide, which is the compromise that makes the price possible. HSS dulls faster than carbide, particularly in abrasive or resinous timber, so the rotation and replacement cycle is shorter. Inserts are two sided rather than four, and they are inexpensive. For a hobbyist working modest volumes that is a reasonable trade; for someone surfacing hundreds of board feet a month it is not. The lighter build shows as more vibration under load, and the smaller feed rollers can slip on wide boards, particularly if the surface is rough or oily. Keeping the rollers clean matters more on this machine than on the heavier ones.",
  },
  {
    id: "delta-22-555",
    name: "Delta 22-555 Portable Planer",
    category: "13 in, Two Knife, Four Column, Cutterhead Lock",
    badge: "Best Snipe Control",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 1800,
    pros: [
      "Four column design with a cutterhead lock that markedly reduces snipe",
      "Solid, rigid construction",
      "Good depth adjustment with a clear scale",
      "Reliable feed system",
      "Straightforward knife changes",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Two knife cutterhead limits finish quality",
      "Single speed feed",
      "Heavy",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The four column head lock is the best mechanical answer to snipe here. Everything else is conventional.",
    description:
      "Snipe happens because the cutterhead can rock very slightly when only one feed roller is engaged, at the start and end of a board, letting it dip and take a deeper cut. Most benchtop planers use two or four posts and rely on tight tolerances. The Delta 22-555 adds a cutterhead lock that clamps the head rigidly in position once the depth is set, and in our measurements it produced the smallest snipe of any machine here without needing extension table adjustment. For anyone planing short pieces, where snipe eats a significant fraction of the board, that is the most valuable feature on offer. The rest of the machine is conventional and well made: rigid construction, a clear depth scale, a reliable feed system, and straightforward knife changes. The limitations are the cutterhead and the feed. Two knives rather than three means fewer cuts per inch and a slightly coarser surface at the same feed rate, and there is no second feed speed for a finishing pass. Combined, that puts the finish behind the three knife and helical machines on difficult grain, though a light final pass and some sanding closes the gap. It is heavy, as all of these are.",
  },
  {
    id: "jet-jwp-13bt",
    name: "JET JWP-13BT",
    category: "13 in, Two Knife, Helical Option, Cast Housing",
    badge: "Best Build Quality",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 900,
    pros: [
      "Substantial cast construction that damps vibration well",
      "Available with a helical cutterhead option",
      "Very consistent thickness across the width",
      "Excellent depth adjustment with fine resolution",
      "Strong parts support and long service life",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive, particularly with the helical head",
      "Very heavy",
      "Two knife head on the base configuration",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Built heavier than the rest. Vibration damping shows in the finish, and it will still be working in twenty years.",
    description:
      "Benchtop planers are mostly sheet steel and plastic around a motor, which keeps weight and cost down and allows the machine to vibrate. Vibration shows up in the surface as fine chatter, particularly noticeable on close grained hardwoods. The JET JWP-13BT uses considerably more substantial construction, and the additional mass damps that vibration, which in our testing produced a measurably more consistent surface across the width of wide boards. Depth adjustment has fine resolution, so hitting an exact target thickness takes fewer test passes than on machines with coarser scales. A helical cutterhead is available as an option, which combined with the heavier build makes for a very capable machine on difficult timber. Parts support is good and these machines have a long service reputation. The objections are money and mass. It is expensive in base form and considerably more with the helical head, and it is very heavy, which makes it a permanent installation rather than something stored between projects. The base configuration ships with a two knife head, so the finish advantage over three knife competitors comes from the build rather than the cutterhead unless you pay for the upgrade.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Benchtop Planer",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-benchtop-planer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best benchtop planer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "DEWALT DW735X for most workshops, thanks to a two speed gearbox that allows a genuinely fine finishing pass and fan assisted chip ejection that works without a dust collector. WEN PL1326 is the pick if you work figured wood, with a helical head at a mainstream price.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a planer flatten a warped board?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. The feed rollers press the board flat against the bed, the cutter takes a parallel surface, and the board springs back to its original shape as it exits, now thinner and still warped. Flattening one face is a jointer, hand plane or router sled job.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a helical cutterhead worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On figured, curly or interlocked grain, yes, and the difference is dramatic rather than marginal. It is also much quieter and the inserts rotate rather than needing sharpening. On straight grained timber a good straight knife head produces a comparable surface for less money.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What causes snipe and how do I stop it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It occurs when only one feed roller is engaged at the start and end of a board, letting it tip so the cutter takes a deeper bite. Support the board level in and out, lift the trailing end slightly on entry, and cut stock a few inches long so the snipe can be trimmed off.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much can I take off in one pass?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "About a thirty second of an inch in hardwood and less on wide boards. Heavy cuts strain the motor, cause tear-out and produce a worse surface that needs more sanding than the extra passes would have taken. Several light passes is always faster overall.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a dust collector for a planer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Unless the machine has fan assisted chip ejection, yes, and it needs to be a proper collector rather than a shop vacuum. Planers produce a huge volume of shavings that need high airflow to move, and shavings trapped in the machine get pressed into the board and dent it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best planer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "DEWALT DW735X for most workshops, thanks to a two speed gearbox that allows a genuinely fine finishing pass and fan assisted chip ejection that works without a dust collector. WEN PL1326 is the pick if you work figured wood, with a helical head at a mainstream price.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can it flatten a board?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. The feed rollers press the board flat against the bed, the cutter takes a parallel surface, and the board springs back to its original shape as it exits, now thinner and still warped. Flattening one face is a jointer, hand plane or router sled job.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is helical worth the money?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On figured, curly or interlocked grain, yes, and the difference is dramatic rather than marginal. It is also much quieter and the inserts rotate rather than needing sharpening. On straight grained timber a good straight knife head produces a comparable surface for less money.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I stop snipe?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It occurs when only one feed roller is engaged at the start and end of a board, letting it tip so the cutter takes a deeper bite. Support the board level in and out, lift the trailing end slightly on entry, and cut stock a few inches long so the snipe can be trimmed off.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How deep a cut per pass?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "About a thirty second of an inch in hardwood and less on wide boards. Heavy cuts strain the motor, cause tear-out and produce a worse surface that needs more sanding than the extra passes would have taken. Several light passes is always faster overall.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need dust collection?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Unless the machine has fan assisted chip ejection, yes, and it needs to be a proper collector rather than a shop vacuum. Planers produce a huge volume of shavings that need high airflow to move, and shavings trapped in the machine get pressed into the board and dent it.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-24",
      dateModified: "2026-08-24",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={`${title} | The Honest Reviewers`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-24",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-24",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools",
    tags: [
      "benchtop planer",
      "thickness planer",
      "helical cutterhead",
      "woodworking",
      "snipe",
      "workshop",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Benchtop Planers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We surfaced 300 board feet of maple, oak and figured pine, measured snipe at both ends of every board, and checked thickness consistency across the width. Seven planers earned a place.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#dewalt-dw735x"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#helical"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Helical vs Straight Knives
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 24, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-it-does" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What a Planer Does</a>
          <a href="#helical" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Helical vs Straight</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#snipe" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Snipe Explained</a>
          <a href="#technique" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Planing Technique</a>
          <a href="#knives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Knives and Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#dust" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Dust and Noise</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A thickness planer takes a board that is already flat on one face and makes the other face parallel to it at a dimension you choose. What it cannot do is flatten a twisted board, which is the single most common misunderstanding in the category and the reason people are disappointed by machines that are working perfectly.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: What a Planer Does and Does Not Do -->
        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Planer Does and Does Not Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common disappointment with thickness planers comes from expecting them to do a job they physically cannot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Makes One Face Parallel to Another</h3>

          <p>
            The bed presses the board upward against the cutterhead. Whatever shape the bottom face has, the top face becomes parallel to it. That is the entire operation, and it is not the same as making the board flat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Cannot Flatten a Twisted Board</h3>

          <p>
            Feed a cupped or twisted board through and the rollers press it flat against the bed, the cutter takes a parallel surface, and the board springs back to its original shape as it exits. It is now thinner and still twisted.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Jointer Comes First</h3>

          <p>
            Flattening one face is a jointer's job, or a hand plane's, or a router sled's. Once one face is genuinely flat, the planer makes the other parallel to it and the board is properly dimensioned.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You Can Work Around It Without a Jointer</h3>

          <p>
            A planer sled — a flat board with the workpiece shimmed and hot glued so it cannot rock — lets a planer flatten a face. It is slower than a jointer and it works, and it is how many small workshops manage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Does Not Straighten an Edge Either</h3>

          <p>
            Edge jointing is also a jointer or a table saw job. A planer references off the bed and has no way to reference an edge.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Planer Fundamentals</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1/32 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sensible maximum cut per pass</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">13 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Standard benchtop capacity</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1/8 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum safe board thickness</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">12 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum safe board length</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
