        <!-- SECTION 2: Helical vs Straight Knives -->
        <div id="helical" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Helical vs Straight Knives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cutterhead type is the biggest single difference between planers and it changes finish, noise and running cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Straight knives chop across the full width at once.</strong> Two or three long blades hit the board simultaneously, which is efficient and violent. On straight grained timber the result is excellent; on figured or interlocked grain it lifts and tears fibres.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Helical heads present small inserts at a shear angle.</strong> Dozens of square carbide cutters arranged in a spiral each take a small slice, cutting rather than chopping. On figured maple, curly grain and interlocked tropical species the difference is dramatic.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Helical heads are much quieter.</strong> Replacing a full width chop with many small slices removes most of the impact noise, and the reduction is substantial enough to change how unpleasant the machine is to be near.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Inserts rotate instead of being sharpened.</strong> A nicked carbide insert is loosened, turned ninety degrees and retightened, which restores a fresh edge. Straight knives must be replaced or sent for sharpening, and a nick anywhere leaves a ridge along every board.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Straight knives are cheaper to buy and to replace.</strong> A set of knives costs far less than a set of carbide inserts, and the initial machine cost is lower. Over a long life the economics favour helical, and up front they do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Retrofit heads exist and are expensive.</strong> Most popular planers can be fitted with an aftermarket helical head, often for a substantial fraction of the machine price. Buying a planer with one fitted is usually better value.
          </p>
        </div>

