        <!-- SECTION 4: Snipe and How to Stop It -->
        <div id="snipe" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Snipe and How to Stop It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Snipe is the shallow depression cut into the first and last few inches of a board, and it is the defining flaw of benchtop planers.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>It happens when only one roller is engaged.</strong> With both feed rollers gripping, the board is held firmly. At entry and exit only one is engaged, letting the board tip very slightly and the cutter take a deeper bite.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Support the board level, in and out.</strong> Infeed and outfeed tables set exactly level with the bed, or roller stands adjusted carefully, remove most of the tipping. A board sagging as it leaves is the most common cause.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Lift the trailing end slightly on entry.</strong> A gentle upward pressure on the end of the board as it enters, and on the leading end as it exits, counteracts the tip. It sounds crude and it works.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Lock the cutterhead if the machine allows.</strong> Four column machines with a head lock clamp the cutterhead rigidly once depth is set, which prevents the rocking that causes snipe in the first place.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Run boards butted end to end.</strong> Feeding a continuous train of boards means each one is supported by the next, so snipe occurs only on the first and last board of the run. This is the standard workshop technique.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Plan for it and cut it off.</strong> Leaving a few extra inches at each end and trimming after planing is the simplest, most reliable solution, and it costs a little material rather than a lot of frustration.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Leave Boards Long, Then Trim</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Every technique for eliminating snipe reduces it rather than removing it entirely. Cutting your stock four to six inches longer than needed, planing, and then trimming the ends off is the only method that works every time on every machine. Experienced woodworkers do this automatically and rarely mention it, which is why beginners think their planer is faulty.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Getting Good Results -->
        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Getting Good Results
          </h2>

          <p>
            A planer is simple to operate and the difference between an acceptable surface and a good one is a handful of habits.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Take light passes.</strong> A thirty second of an inch is a sensible maximum in hardwood, less on wide boards. Heavy cuts strain the motor, cause tear-out, and produce a worse surface that then needs more sanding than the passes you saved.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Read the grain and feed accordingly.</strong> Grain that rises toward the trailing end of the board tears out. Turning the board end for end so the cutter works with the grain rather than against it removes most tear-out without any other change.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Alternate faces to keep the board balanced.</strong> Removing material from one side only releases internal stresses unevenly and can cause the board to cup. Taking passes from both faces alternately keeps it stable.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Let it acclimatise before final dimensioning.</strong> Timber moves as its moisture content equilibrates with your workshop. Planing to final thickness immediately after bringing wood in means the parts will move afterwards. Rough it close, wait a week, then finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never plane below the minimum thickness.</strong> Below roughly an eighth of an inch the board can be caught by the cutterhead and destroyed, taking your hands toward the machine. Glue thin stock to a carrier board instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check for metal before every board.</strong> A nail, a staple or grit embedded in reclaimed timber will nick a knife or destroy inserts across the whole width. A metal detector is cheap compared with a set of knives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Knives, Inserts and Keeping the Edge -->
        <div id="knives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Knives, Inserts and Keeping the Edge
          </h2>

          <p>
            A planer is only as good as the edge it presents, and a dull or nicked cutter shows on every board that follows.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A nick shows as a ridge on every board.</strong> Because a straight knife spans the whole width, a single chip in the edge leaves a raised line down the length of everything you plane afterwards. It is the most visible failure in the category and it is caused almost entirely by embedded grit and fasteners.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Double edged knives can be flipped once.</strong> Most benchtop planer knives are reversible, giving a second life for the cost of a knife change. Note which edge has been used, because it is very easy to reinstall a dull edge and conclude the machine has a problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Carbide inserts rotate four times.</strong> A helical head insert is square and indexed, so a nicked one is loosened, turned and retightened. Only the damaged insert needs attention rather than a whole set, which is the real running cost advantage of the format.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Set knives with the supplied gauge, not by eye.</strong> Knives sitting at different heights produce an uneven cut and increase chatter. Every machine ships with a setting jig, and using it properly is what separates a good change from a frustrating one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Torque the knife screws in the specified sequence.</strong> Tightening from one end can shift the knife as you go. Working from the centre outwards, in stages, keeps it seated where the gauge put it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep the bed waxed and the rollers clean.</strong> Friction on the bed makes the feed rollers work harder and can cause stalling or slipping mid-board. A coat of paste wax on the bed and a wipe of the rollers takes five minutes and removes most feed problems.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Dust, Noise and Workshop Practicalities -->
        <div id="dust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Dust, Noise and Workshop Practicalities
          </h2>

          <p>
            Planers produce more waste per minute than any other workshop machine, and that has practical consequences beyond tidiness.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Shavings arrive in enormous volume.</strong> A single wide board can fill a bin, and a machine without a collector attached will fill its own housing within a few passes. This is not comparable to sawdust from a saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Blocked shavings damage the workpiece.</strong> Shavings that cannot escape get pressed between the feed roller and the board, denting the freshly cut surface. This is the most common cause of mysterious marks on planed timber.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Fan assisted ejection makes a collector optional.</strong> Machines with a built in impeller blow shavings clear under their own power, which means a bin and a hose is sufficient. Machines without one need a proper dust collector, not a shop vacuum.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A shop vacuum is the wrong tool.</strong> Vacuums provide high suction at low volume, and planer shavings need high volume at low suction. A vacuum clogs almost immediately and its filter blinds.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hearing protection is not optional.</strong> Straight knife planers are among the loudest tools in any workshop, well into the range that causes cumulative damage. Muffs rather than plugs, and worn for the entire run.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Think about where it lives.</strong> A planer needs clear space in front and behind equal to the length of the longest board you will run. In a small workshop that usually means positioning it near a door or a window that can be opened.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best planer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                DEWALT DW735X for most workshops, thanks to a two speed gearbox that allows a genuinely fine finishing pass and fan assisted chip ejection that works without a dust collector. WEN PL1326 is the pick if you work figured wood, with a helical head at a mainstream price.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can it flatten a board?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The feed rollers press the board flat against the bed, the cutter takes a parallel surface, and the board springs back to its original shape as it exits, now thinner and still warped. Flattening one face is a jointer, hand plane or router sled job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is helical worth the money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On figured, curly or interlocked grain, yes, and the difference is dramatic rather than marginal. It is also much quieter and the inserts rotate rather than needing sharpening. On straight grained timber a good straight knife head produces a comparable surface for less money.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop snipe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It occurs when only one feed roller is engaged at the start and end of a board, letting it tip so the cutter takes a deeper bite. Support the board level in and out, lift the trailing end slightly on entry, and cut stock a few inches long so the snipe can be trimmed off.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How deep a cut per pass?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About a thirty second of an inch in hardwood and less on wide boards. Heavy cuts strain the motor, cause tear-out and produce a worse surface that needs more sanding than the extra passes would have taken. Several light passes is always faster overall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need dust collection?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Unless the machine has fan assisted chip ejection, yes, and it needs to be a proper collector rather than a shop vacuum. Planers produce a huge volume of shavings that need high airflow to move, and shavings trapped in the machine get pressed into the board and dent it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Planer Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most planer disappointment comes from asking the machine to do a job it cannot, or from rushing the job it can.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting It to Flatten Warped Stock</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The rollers press a cupped or twisted board flat against the bed, the cutter takes a parallel surface, and the board springs back on exit — thinner and still warped. One face has to be flattened first, on a jointer, with a hand plane, or with a planer sled that stops the board rocking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking Too Heavy a Cut</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Deep passes strain the motor, tear out the grain and leave a surface that needs far more sanding than the passes you thought you were saving. A thirty second of an inch is a sensible maximum in hardwood, and less on wide boards where the cutter is working across more material.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Planning for Snipe</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every technique reduces snipe rather than eliminating it. Cutting stock four to six inches longer than the finished part, then trimming the ends after planing, is the only approach that works every time. Beginners who skip this conclude their machine is faulty when it is behaving normally.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Reclaimed Timber Without Checking It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A nail, a staple, or embedded grit will nick a straight knife or shatter carbide inserts across the entire width, leaving a ridge on every board planed afterwards. A cheap metal detector costs a fraction of a set of knives and takes seconds per board.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

